// GAP-ZYB-006 — a `<~` at the top level ends the program, and its value is the
// exit status.
//
// No new symbol was needed, which is the point. SYMBOLS.md § 17 rule 1 says
// derive rather than invent: `<~` hands a value back to whoever called, and a
// program is called by the operating system, so a value handed back at the top
// level IS the exit status. Rule 7 asks for the iconic form over the
// conventional one, and this is the same return arrow the reader already knows.
//
// It also closed a live divergence nobody had reported, because nobody writes
// `<~` in a file body: the register VM and the browser engine already stopped
// the program there, and the tree-walker walked past and ran the rest of the
// file. The same program printed different things under `--vm`.
//
// Why it had never come up: every suite in this project decides by comparing
// output. A project that only ever tests by golden never misses an exit code.
//
// The `<~` here is inside a `?`, which is where it belongs — a program that
// always exits at the same place has nothing to report.
codigo = 0

>> "empieza" ¶

? codigo == 99 {
    >> "no se toma" ¶
    <~ 1
}

>> "sigue" ¶

// A value that is not a whole number is refused before the program runs — the
// exit status is a number for the operating system, and a text turned into one
// in silence is the kind of conversion this language avoids. See
// errors/semantic/salida_no_entera.zy.

// Falling off the end is exit 0, unchanged, and this file does exactly that:
// a corpus case has to be comparable, and a non-zero exit would make the
// runner report the program as failed.
>> "termina" ¶
